is the Liouville form on $TM$ and is a K\"ahler form. The corresponding K\"ahler metric induces the original metric $g$ along the zero section $M$.

This induces a conjugation $\tilde{\sigma}$ on $T^{1,0}G_{\mathbb{C}}$ given by

\begin{equation} T^{1,0}G_{\mathbb{C}} \ni \zeta \to \tilde{\sigma}(\zeta) := \overline{D\sigma(\zeta)} \in T^{1,0}G_{\mathbb{C}}.
\end{equation}

Consider the manifold $G \times S^1$, where $G$ is given the bi-invariant metric of sectional curvature 1, which is the special case of (\ref{eqn:U2sym}) with $\lambda_1= 1$, and the $S^1$ factor is given the metric $\mu d\theta^2,$ where $d\theta^2$ is the invariant metric of length $2\pi$ on $S^1$ and $\mu$ is a real constant $> 0$. So $G\times S^1$ has the metric $g_{\mu}$ defined by

\begin{equation} g_{\mu} = g_I \oplus \mu \, d\theta^2.
\end{equation}

We want to see which $\lambda$ in (\ref{eqn:U2sym}) will arise from Sz\H{o}ke's construction. Computing at $(e,1) \in G\times S^1$, the tangent vector to the $S^1$ action we will quotient by is given by $(2\xi_3, \frac{\partial \;\;}{\partial \theta})$. The projection of $(\xi_3, 0)$ onto this direction is given by

\begin{equation} \begin{array}{rcl}
	(\xi_3,0)^T & = & \frac{((\xi_3, 0), (\xi_3, \frac{\partial \;\;}{\partial \theta}))}{|\xi_3 + \frac{\partial \;\;}{\partial \theta}|^2} (\xi_3, \frac{\partial \;\;}{\partial \theta}), \\
	& & \\
	& = & \frac2{2+\mu} (\xi_3, \frac{\partial \;\;}{\partial \theta})
	\end{array}

\end{equation}

and hence the normal component is given by 

\begin{equation} \begin{array}{rcl}
		(\xi_3,0)^N & = & (\xi_3,0) - (\xi_3,0)^T \\
		&	&	\\
		& = & (\frac{\mu}{2+\mu} \xi_3, \frac2{2+\mu} \frac{\partial \;\;}{\partial \theta}). 	
	\end{array}

\end{equation}

Thus, 

\[
	|\xi_3^N|^2 = 2 \frac{\mu}{2+\mu},
\]

which means that the quotient metric on $G$ is $g_A$ with $A$ in (\ref{eqn:A}) having $\lambda = \frac{\mu}{2 + \mu}.$

